NAME
    passwd - change a password

SYNOPSIS
    passwd [user]

DESCRIPTION
    Changes a password. With no argument, changes your own; with a name, that
    account's.

    ANY USER may change their OWN password. Only root may change someone
    else's. A non-root caller is asked for their current password first and
    must get it right; root is not asked at all.

    The new password may not be empty. useradd enforces the same rule on the
    initial one.

    passwd itself has no privilege and writes nothing. It prompts, then asks
    the ACCOUNT SERVER (accountd) to make the change. The server asks the
    kernel who sent the message - a binding a program cannot forge - and
    applies the policy above. So running passwd grants nothing by itself; the
    decision is made somewhere the caller cannot reach.

    passwd derives the new secret itself (version 2: PBKDF2-HMAC-SHA-512 over
    a fresh 7-byte salt, about a second on the QEMU guest) and sends only
    hashes; no password leaves this process. Secrets are stored in
    /etc/shadow, never in the world-readable /etc/passwd (see login for the
    line formats).

    THE CLUSTER KEY CHANGES WITH THE PASSWORD. On a node with a cluster realm
    (/etc/cluster/realm), passwd then derives the account's new cluster key
    (another second) and prints its /etc/cluster/users line. Every export
    that lists the user there refuses them until that line is replaced with
    the new one, and a login that was already running still holds the old
    key until it logs in again. With no realm, no line is printed; when the
    line cannot be made for another reason (the realm or /etc/passwd
    unreadable, a name a line cannot carry), passwd says so, and
    `clusterkey user <name>` prints it later.

    THAT FILE IS ONLY PROTECTED ON ext2. Its mode is 0600 root, but FAT32 and
    exFAT record no mode at all, so the filesystem server has nothing to
    enforce: on those volumes any user can read the hashes AND overwrite
    root's entry with their own. This is not new - /etc/passwd was equally
    writable there before the secrets moved out - and it cannot be fixed on a
    filesystem with no permission model. login says so at the prompt when it
    detects one. Use ext2 for anything where the accounts are meant to hold. The
    salt comes from the machine's hardware RNG where there is one; without it
    the server falls back to a weaker clock-derived salt and says so on the
    console.

    If ACCOUNTD.BIN is not staged, passwd reports that there is no account
    server rather than pretending to succeed.

EXAMPLES
    $ passwd                        change your own
    Current password: ****
    New password: ****
    Retype new password: ****
    passwd: checking the current password...    (a version-2 secret)
    passwd: deriving the new password's hash...
    passwd: password updated
    passwd: deriving the cluster key...
    passwd: the cluster key changed with the password. Every export that lists
      this user in /etc/cluster/users refuses them until that line is replaced by:
    user 5d0c...9a41

    # passwd user                   root changing another account
    New password: ****
    Retype new password: ****
    passwd: deriving the new password's hash...
    passwd: password updated
    passwd: deriving the cluster key...
    passwd: the cluster key changed with the password. Every export that lists
      this user in /etc/cluster/users refuses them until that line is replaced by:
    user 7b21...04ce

DIAGNOSTICS
    "current password is wrong"                  the old password didn't verify
    "only root may change another user's password"
    "no such user"                               no such account in /etc/passwd

SEE ALSO
